-- title:
--   Non-vanishing of the pencil j + c j_q - a on X₀(q)
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime number and let $c, a$ be arbitrary elements of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (taken as `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`). The ambient field is `modularFunctionFieldBar (1 * q)`, the intermediate field of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}((t))$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ generated by the image, under the coefficientwise map `coeffEmb` induced by $\mathbb{Q} \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, of the full modular function field of level $1 \cdot q$; that is, the base change to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the function field of $X_0(q)$, realised inside Laurent series. In it, `jFun` is the element whose underlying series is `coeffEmb` applied to the $q$-expansion `jq` of the modular invariant $j$, and `jqFun` is the element whose underlying series is `coeffEmb` applied to `qExpand ℚ (1 * q) jq`, the series obtained from `jq` by multiplying all exponents by $q$, i.e. the expansion of $j(q\tau)$. The assertion is that the element
--   $$\mathrm{jFun} + c \cdot \mathrm{jqFun} - a$$
--   is non-zero in this field, where $c$ and $a$ enter through the structure map $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} \to$ `modularFunctionFieldBar (1 * q)` embedding constants. No hypothesis such as $c \neq 0$ is imposed, and the level is kept in the unnormalised form $1 \cdot q$.
--
--   This is the non-degeneracy statement that the pencil of modular functions $j + c\,j_q$ takes no constant value identically on $X_0(q)_{\overline{\mathbb{Q}}}$; such a function has a pole at one of the two cusps, whereas a constant has order zero everywhere. It is used as the non-vanishing hypothesis in [`ModularCurve.PlaceSpecialization.LevelOneProlongationPair.sum_ord_pencil_eq`](thm.html#ModularCurve.PlaceSpecialization.LevelOneProlongationPair.sum_ord_pencil_eq), where orders at places of the divisor of $j + c\,j_q - a$ are summed.
